# Security (Universal)

## Secrets & config

- Never hardcode passwords, API keys, tokens, or private keys. Use environment variables or a secrets manager.
- Never commit `.env`, credentials files, or private certs. Warn if asked to commit them.
- Rotate exposed secrets immediately; do not log secrets or full auth headers.

## Authentication & authorization

- Authenticate every protected endpoint. Default to **deny** — opt in to public access explicitly.
- Authorize every action: check the user/role owns or may access the resource (IDOR prevention).
- Use framework-native auth (sessions, JWT validation, OAuth) — do not roll custom crypto.
- Hash passwords with bcrypt/argon2/scrypt — never plain text or reversible encoding.

## Input & output

- Treat all external input as untrusted: query params, headers, body, file uploads, webhooks.
- Use parameterized queries / ORM — **never** concatenate user input into SQL or shell commands.
- Escape or sanitize output for the context (HTML, URL, SQL, shell). Prevent XSS and injection.
- Validate type, length, range, and format server-side — client validation is not security.
- Limit upload size and file types; scan or sandbox where appropriate.

## API & network

- HTTPS in production. Secure cookies: `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite` where applicable.
- Rate-limit auth and sensitive endpoints. Return generic errors on login failure (no user enumeration).
- Set security headers (CSP, X-Frame-Options, etc.) for web apps.
- CORS: allow only required origins — not `*` with credentials.

## Data & privacy

- Collect and store only data you need. Encrypt sensitive data at rest when required.
- Audit access to PII and financial data. Log security events without logging sensitive payloads.
